  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1039 PM CST FRI NOV 27 2026\n\nGOES TPW imagery reveals PWATs down to 0.3-0.4\" across Central\nAlabama. This influx of drier air will help keep us dry through\nthe first half of the weekend. Light winds, clear skies, and dry\nair will allow temps to plummet tonight with temps from the mid\n20s to low 30s to start our Saturday morning. We look to add on\nabout 5-10 degrees on Saturday as southerly flow returns. This\nwill also begin to usher Gulf moisture back across the Southeast\nin advance of our next storm system. By Sunday, an upper trough\nwill dig across the Plains and lift across the Southeast. An\nassociated cold front will swing through the region Sunday\nafternoon through early Monday morning. Winds will pick up ahead\nof the front as the pressure gradient tightens with gusts up to\n30 knots likely. If this trends up, a Wind Advisory will likely\nbe needed. We will continue with medium to high chances for rain\nSunday into early Monday morning as this feature moves through.\nInstability has trended up slightly with recent model runs with\nCAPE values topping out in the 200-400 J/kg range. This will be\nenough to warrant a low chance for a strong to marginally severe\nthunderstorm. As a result, SPC has introduced a marginal (Level\n1 out of 5) risk on Sunday for much of the northern half of\nAlabama.\n \nCooler and drier weather is expected early next week as surface\nhigh pressure takes control. We gradually warm back up through\nthe week as southerly flow returns. Another cold front heads our\nway towards the end of the work week, bringing increased rain\nchances back to the region.\n\n95/Castillo",
